L000216	
L000217	\section{Marchesini-Onofri equation near criticality}
L000218	
L000219	For the SU$(N)$ invariant (singlet) sector of MQM, it is well known \cite{Brezin:1977sv} that the system is described by $N$ non interacting fermions. The ground state is obtained by filling the first $N$ energy levels in potential $V$.
L000220	In the WKB approximation, the singlet excitation gap is given by the classical frequency $\omega(g)$
L000221	\begin{equation}\label{frequency}
L000222	     \frac{2\pi}{\omega(g)}=\frac{1}{\pi}\oint\limits\frac{\d x}{\rho(x)}, \qquad \rho(x) = \frac{1}{\pi}\sqrt{2(\mu_F-V(x))}\ .\\
L000223	\end{equation}
L000224	Here $\rho$ is the eigenvalue density (or the fermion density \cite{Brezin:1977sv}) in the ground state, which is supported between $x_1$ and $x_2$, the classical turning points of the potential $V(x)$ (for even potentials $x_2=-x_1=a$).
L000225	For the first non-trivial representation of SU$(N)$, which is the adjoint representation, the situation is more complicated. In \cite{Marchesini:1979yq}, it was shown that the spectrum of the adjoint sector, in the large-$N$ limit, is structured as follows: for each singlet state with energy $E_{s}^{\rm singlet}$, there is an infinite tower of bound states in the adjoint sector with energies $E_{(s,n)}^{\rm adjoint}$
L000226	\begin{equation}
L000227	    E_{(s,n)}^{\rm adjoint}(g)=E_s^{\rm singlet}(g)+\Delta_n(g)\ ,\quad n=1,2,\dots
L000228	\end{equation}
L000229	The structure of this tower does not depend on the choice of the $s$-th singlet state: $\Delta_n(g)$ is a universal gap between the $n$-th state in the tower and the singlet state.
L000230	
L000231	
L000232	To study these gaps in the large $N$ limit, Marchesini and Onofri \cite{Marchesini:1979yq} derived the singular integral equation
L000233	\begin{equation}\label{MO-eq}
L000234	    \Delta_n\, \Phi_n(x)=\fint_{x_1}^{x_2}\limits \d y\, \rho(y)
L000235	    \frac{\Phi_n(x)-\Phi_n(y)}{(x-y)^2}\ ,
L000236	\end{equation}
L000237	where
L000238	$\Phi_n(x)$ are the eigenfunctions associated with the corresponding adjoint states.
L000239	
L000240	The MO equation \eqref{MO-eq} resembles the well-known 't Hooft equation \cite{THOOFT1974461}, which describes mesons in two-dimensional large $N$ QCD. In fact, for $V(x)=0$, the MO equation becomes equivalent to the 't Hooft equation for massless quarks, and $\Delta$ is then proportional to the meson mass-squared. In this case, the spectrum consists of two Regge trajectories of mesons \cite{Fateev:2009jf,Litvinov:2024riz,Artemev:2025cev}. This is qualitatively similar to the two Regge trajectories \eqref{eq:regge-phi4}.
L000241	For the 't Hooft model the trajectories are asymptotically linear, and their string theoretic interpretation is in terms of longitudinal oscillations of 2D open strings with moving endpoints \cite{Bardeen:1975gx}.
L000242	
L000243	The eigenfunctions $\Phi_n$, $n=1, 2, \ldots$, are orthonormal with respect to the measure $\rho(x) \d x$, e.g.,
L000244	 $\int_{x_1}^{x_2}\limits \d x\, \rho(x) \, \Phi^*_n(x)\Phi_m(x)=\delta_{nm}\, $
L000245	while the adjoint representation constraint \cite{Marchesini:1979yq,Maldacena:2005hi}
L000246	    $\int_{x_1}^{x_2}\limits \d x\, \rho(x)  \, \Phi_n(x)=0 $
L000247	removes the constant eigenfunction with eigenvalue $0$. The lowest eigenvalue $\Delta_1$ corresponds to $\Phi_1(x)$, which is an odd function for the quartic potential \eqref{eq:quartpot}.
L000248	
L000249	After introducing $\phi_n(x)=\rho(x) \,\Phi_n(x)$, the MO equation becomes
L000250	$\Delta_n\,\phi_n(x)=\mathcal{H}\,\phi_n(x)$. The effective Hamiltonian $\mathcal{H}$ is defined by \cite{Marchesini:1979yq}
L000251	\begin{align}\label{MO-1}
L000252	    \mathcal{H}\,\phi_n(x)=-\rho(x)\fint_{x_1}^{x_2}\limits \d y\,\frac{\phi_n(y)}{(x-y)^2}
L000253	    + \eta(g,x)\phi_n(x)
L000254	    \ ,
L000255	\end{align}
L000256	where $\eta(\mu,x)$ defined below plays the role of ``potential,''
L000257	\begin{equation}\label{MO-potential}
L000258	    \eta(g,x)\equiv\fint_{x_1}^{x_2}\limits \d y\,\frac{\rho(y)}{(x-y)^2}\ , \qquad \eta(g)=\frac{\omega(g)}{\pi}\int_{x_1}^{x_2}\limits\frac{\d x}{\pi}\frac{\eta(g,x)}{\rho(x)}\ ,
L000259	\end{equation}
